ave  a weekly call with a medical advisory  committee group we've been working with  several months now and those are medical  leaders tied either to their athletics  program in the university so we'll  receive updates there and and again I  think that's where it's important to  understand their testing outcomes and  then there is essentially tracing  outcomes where because of potential  contacts quarantine takes place even  though someone may not have tested  positive for Kovac 19 what kind of  options are you looking at for the  season yeah I describe this as a  marathon and really I caught the end of  your previous segment on the returned  activities that may or may not happen at  major league baseball and other sports I  know they're all preparing for that  we're living that reality we're dealing  literally with voluntary athletic  related activities and we know that  within our facilities we can provide the  kind of care and oversight that guides  people in the right way keeps them  healthy we're also learning that 19 and  20 year olds will do what 19 and 20 year  olds do and that is maybe not listen to  all the advice that exists I think their  immunity level or their their strength  and conditioning can prevent them from  being having a positive test or  contracting a virus and I think that's a  lesson that everyone still has to learn  that the discussions about masks and how  we maintain our health these are real  issues that help us move forward or  we're still months away from what could  be the potential kickoff that's our  focus but as I've said repeatedly we  have to prepare to adjust to the  circumstances just like we're adjusting  right now after the last four or five  days what will those adjustment wait  what are the possible and maybe you  don't want to get into hypotheticals to  be fair to you but I everybody's  optimistic about this Commissioner but  man I I just I don't know I have serious  doubts about how we're gonna pull all  any of this off I really am  nobody like nobody on my side of the  microphone when you're asked questions  has been overly optimistic and I'm  certainly realistic it is it is a  marathon and I don't know where the  finish line is and in my life I finished  41 marathons and at least I knew with  the exception of one where they  measured that the finish line was 26.2  miles you can imagine my frustration out  I knew it felt longer that was and here  we know there's a finish line but where  is it and so then I have to look at this  as each step and a couple of weeks ago  we opened up our athletic facilities  again voluntary activities what you're  seeing now in the news is a reaction to  the plans that were put in place today  is another milestone the NCAA made a  decision to allow an elevated level of  practice I'll call it come July 3rd I  think that's the next stage and right  around that time we'll have to come to  some conclusions on whether we can move  forward as planned or not was this  planned or this was expected that you  would have these kids test positive well  it was prepared for the goal was never  to expect 0 that's just not reality  around us it is the Binet the plan was  test when they arrived we had positive  Testament people came back and we have  embedded medical professionals in our  athletics program that's part of what I  think gets missed is the level of care  provided for young people on our  campuses on a daily basis and then there  was preparation for the what ifs and so  those hypotheticals that I have avoided  answering are very much a part of  planning what do we do if bunch of  people go to a party where someone tests  positive you're going to be in  quarantine what do we do if there's a  positive test what do we do and they  come back how many people can be allowed  in a facility what is the spacing what  is the expectation when you walk in and  leave what is the continuing  reinforcement about the education  provided that well you made it through  one week doesn't make you make doesn't  mean you'll make it through two unless  you take care of yourselves and there  are no guarantee if you can go back to  March where we all thought we were  playing basketball tournaments at March  Madness what happened we made  assumptions and we have fewer  assumptions made and that's why we have  to prepare for a whole set of  contingencies we talked about it last  time and like if you can give me an  update with the SCC maybe just have  conference games like the is the game  plan to have just  Conference games or is it a full slate  we've we've gone through I think we're  just short of twenty scenarios the the  plan the focus is to prepare the play as  scheduled and then to pivot off of that  and there are any number of options that  could allow an entire schedule to be  played both conference non conference  but everybody in this country has to be  a part of that at the collegiate level  that's just a little bit more complex  than say the NFL putting its schedule  out there delaying because all of those  things are controlled by a central  entity so I think we'll keep that focus  but we'll plan for contingencies that  could be you know probably anything you  read about from a speculative standpoint  maybe just Alabama and LSU play 12 times  you know I can take one of those a year  I imagine the stress level in my life  bearings right now it's not summer  commissioner thank you we appreciate  your time and good luck well I  appreciate the continued conversation  thank you it's a Greg Sankey the  Commissioner of the SEC

